al_speaker_profile_id":"james-a-garfield","appearance_id":"appearance_103b5996e1ae073abcd31bc271dc5c95566fcc0840e658331e98571b2b10fc82","cross_owner":false,"doc_name":"/the-presidency/presidential-speeches/march-4-1881-inaugural-address","edge_id":"edge_0479820aee23f4898f0bd34e577c4bc02aa7131960d129172a9cdc8df588b2f4","evidence_excerpt":"The finances of the Government shall suffer no detriment which it maybe possible for my Administration to prevent. The interests of agriculture deserve more attention from the Government than they have yet received. The farms of the United States afford homes and employment for more than one-half our people, and furnish much the largest part of all our expo…","para_idx":22,"receipt_id":"receipt_2564facd6f36c2f8e78576f992e8524eb43f879527afc37de3a9bff172036fcc","selection_position":0,"selection_role":"first","source_document_owner":"James A. Garfield","source_document_owner_profile_id":"james-a-garfield","source_url":"https://millercenter.org/the-presidency/presidential-speeches/march-4-1881-inaugural-address","speech_date":"1881-03-04","speech_title":"March 4, 1881: Inaugural Address","story_era_id":"gilded-age","story_era_label":"Administrative-industrial order"},{"actual_speaker":"James A. Garfield","actual_speaker_profile_id":"james-a-garfield","appearance_id":"appearance_103b5996e1ae073abcd31bc271dc5c95566fcc0840e658331e98571b2b10fc82","cross_owner":false,"doc_name":"/the-presidency/presidential-speeches/march-4-1881-inaugural-address","edge_id":"edge_0a724d72fe3c9ec9f876e4672ba6efe58c2ed1c6a5ef78827cc70d986d88214d","evidence_excerpt":"Our manufacturers are rapidly making us industrially independent, and are opening to capital and labor new and profitable fields of employment.Their steady and healthy growth should still be matured. Our facilities for transportation should be promoted by the continued improvement of our harbors and great interior waterways and by the increase of our tonnag…","para_idx":23,"receipt_id":"receipt_8d90852c7c5462c5f74bbbd98ad287688168bedf4fb043c46947ecc2d06172fd","selection_position":0,"selection_role":"first","source_document_owner":"James A. Garfield","source_document_owner_profile_id":"james-a-garfield","source_url":"https://millercenter.org/the-presidency/presidential-speeches/march-4-1881-inaugural-address","speech_date":"1881-03-04","speech_title":"March 4, 1881: Inaugural Address","story_era_id":"gilded-age","story_era_label":"Administrative-industrial order"},{"actual_speaker":"James A. Garfield","actual_speaker_profile_id":"james-a-garfield","appearance_id":"appearance_103b5996e1ae073abcd31bc271dc5c95566fcc0840e658331e98571b2b10fc82","cross_owner":false,"doc_name":"/the-presidency/presidential-speeches/march-4-1881-inaugural-address","edge_id":"edge_1d3194377437a99aa6df9c0736215dad1414f2f18bfe52c068d8fb58fe845995","evidence_excerpt":"The Constitution guarantees absolute religious freedom. Congress is prohibited from making any law respecting an establishment of religion or prohibiting the free exercise thereof. The Territories of the UnitedStates are subject to the direct legislative authority of Congress, and hence the General Government is responsible for any violation of the Constitu…","para_idx":24,"receipt_id":"receipt_18d8e489fec091060f2d9453d1f7e0b1697ecb01775afd22b3b2211c2142b832","selection_position":0,"selection_role":"first","source_document_owner":"James A. Garfield","source_document_owner_profile_id":"james-a-garfield","source_url":"https://millercenter.org/the-presidency/presidential-speeches/march-4-1881-inaugural-address","speech_date":"1881-03-04","speech_title":"March 4, 1881: Inaugural Address","story_era_id":"gilded-age","story_era_label":"Administrative-industrial order"},{"actual_speaker":"James A. Garfield","actual_speaker_profile_id":"james-a-garfield","appearance_id":"appearance_103b5996e1ae073abcd31bc271dc5c95566fcc0840e658331e98571b2b10fc82","cross_owner":false,"doc_name":"/the-presidency/presidential-speeches/march-4-1881-inaugural-address","edge_id":"edge_33ade628be8517734122ec5c10f411563c726591a1b09c56a33e393132580e34","evidence_excerpt":"Enterprises of the highest importance to our moral and material well-being unite us and offer ample employment of our best powers. Let all our people,leaving behind them the battlefields of dead issues, move forward and in their strength of liberty and the restored Union win the grander victories of peace. The prosperity which now prevails is without parall…","para_idx":18,"receipt_id":"receipt_7a9d2b07f78a9c2c7fd65a7440e4d7d2bfc8a73c2cc7a57b1c5ffd8d2e715d0f","selection_position":0,"selection_role":"first","source_document_owner":"James A. Garfield","source_document_owner_profile_id":"james-a-garfield","source_url":"https://millercenter.org/the-presidency/presidential-speeches/march-4-1881-inaugural-address","speech_date":"1881-03-04","speech_title":"March 4, 1881: Inaugural Address","story_era_id":"gilded-age","story_era_label":"Administrative-industrial order"},{"actual_speaker":"James A. Garfield","actual_speaker_profile_id":"james-a-garfield","appearance_id":"appearance_103b5996e1ae073abcd31bc271dc5c95566fcc0840e658331e98571b2b10fc82","cross_owner":false,"doc_name":"/the-presidency/presidential-speeches/march-4-1881-inaugural-address","edge_id":"edge_3a4e9c82fa089b76b3afe1c9a566e9508a017e8f5470cb68a3cb47e12ff0e6c0","evidence_excerpt":"Fellow-Citizens: We stand to-day upon an eminence which overlooks a hundred years of national life--a century crowded with perils, but crowned with the triumphs of liberty and law. Before continuing the onward march let us pause on this height for a moment to strengthen our faith and renew our hope bya glance at the pathway along which our people have trave…","para_idx":0,"receipt_id":"receipt_02348baf1d8e93bec8a831d7032248c66ef67137a14284e51b2e045a73e9a915","selection_position":0,"selection_role":"first","source_document_owner":"James A. Garfield","source_document_owner_profile_id":"james-a-garfield","source_url":"https://millercenter.org/the-presidency/presidential-speeches/march-4-1881-inaugural-address","speech_date":"1881-03-04","speech_title":"March 4, 1881: Inaugural Address","story_era_id":"gilded-age","story_era_label":"Administrative-industrial order"},{"actual_speaker":"James A. Garfield","actual_speaker_profile_id":"james-a-garfield","appearance_id":"appearance_103b5996e1ae073abcd31bc271dc5c95566fcc0840e658331e98571b2b10fc82","cross_owner":false,"doc_name":"/the-presidency/presidential-speeches/march-4-1881-inaugural-address","edge_id":"edge_6463c5bcc1c114a4d7d28193d69f9987b6c86326de8667f2be38b29f8e464091","evidence_excerpt":"It is now three days more than a hundred years since the adoption of the first written constitution of the United States--the Articles of Confederation and Perpetual Union. The new Republic was then beset with danger on every hand. It had not conquered a place in the family of nations. The decisive battle of the war for independence, whose centennial annive…","para_idx":1,"receipt_id":"receipt_289e5dc519461c532f321925245b219b798bc1d8e3d83520c8d876ccf7cceb19","selection_position":0,"selection_role":"first","source_document_owner":"James A. Garfield","source_document_owner_profile_id":"james-a-garfield","source_url":"https://millercenter.org/the-presidency/presidential-speeches/march-4-1881-inaugural-address","speech_date":"1881-03-04","speech_title":"March 4, 1881: Inaugural Address","story_era_id":"gilded-age","story_era_label":"Administrative-industrial order"},{"actual_speaker":"James A. Garfield","actual_speaker_profile_id":"james-a-garfield","appearance_id":"appearance_103b5996e1ae073abcd31bc271dc5c95566fcc0840e658331e98571b2b10fc82","cross_owner":false,"doc_name":"/the-presidency/presidential-speeches/march-4-1881-inaugural-address","edge_id":"edge_8076971c596f3b187016e8c5610c956ab6618119582ca62fc9da234a29474191","evidence_excerpt":"And now, fellow-citizens, I am about to assume the great trust which you have committed to my hands. I appeal to you for that earnest and thoughtful support which makes this Government in fact, as it is in law, a government of the people. I shall greatly rely upon the wisdom and patriotism of Congress and of those who may share with me the responsibilities…","para_idx":28,"receipt_id":"receipt_1781a415d822d8e816dbc6c7d0d4a6c772b479935ff5fdcd3e4f0650461eb355","selection_position":0,"selection_role":"first","source_document_owner":"James A. Garfield","source_document_owner_profile_id":"james-a-garfield","source_url":"https://millercenter.org/the-presidency/presidential-speeches/march-4-1881-inaugural-address","speech_date":"1881-03-04","speech_title":"March 4, 1881: Inaugural Address","story_era_id":"gilded-age","story_era_label":"Administrative-industrial order"},{"actual_speaker":"James A. Garfield","actual_speaker_profile_id":"james-a-garfield","appearance_id":"appearance_103b5996e1ae073abcd31bc271dc5c95566fcc0840e658331e98571b2b10fc82","cross_owner":false,"doc_name":"/the-presidency/presidential-speeches/march-4-1881-inaugural-address","edge_id":"edge_c5ad97fef83d783a7dcf965dfc0865bf4ce93603ebbe6a2e75e0c36385b1d15d","evidence_excerpt":"Our manufacturers are rapidly making us industrially independent, and are opening to capital and labor new and profitable fields of employment.Their steady and healthy growth should still be matured. Our facilities for transportation should be promoted by the continued improvement of our harbors and great interior waterways and by the increase of our tonnag…","para_idx":23,"receipt_id":"receipt_0f0d0b1515fc4b748091d4e5aba2d7ba4f8bf2d7eff014cf4fe4324c9bebb442","selection_position":0,"selection_role":"first","source_document_owner":"James A. Garfield","source_document_owner_profile_id":"james-a-garfield","source_url":"https://millercenter.org/the-presidency/presidential-speeches/march-4-1881-inaugural-address","speech_date":"1881-03-04","speech_title":"March 4, 1881: Inaugural Address","story_era_id":"gilded-age","story_era_label":"Administ